Squark: msync wakes one single-object waiter for an operation that frees one unit (records 25.73)

ReleaseSemaphore woke every thread sleeping on the semaphore (ULF_WAKE_ALL); one took it and the rest
went back to sleep. Now ReleaseSemaphore(1), SetEvent on an auto-reset event and ReleaseMutex wake one
of them. A release of more than one still wakes all: a wake call costs the releasing thread the same
for one thread or all, so waking n one by one made MSFS 2024's main thread's ReleaseSemaphore(7) cost
3.7 µs instead of 0.7 µs. Manual-reset events, PulseEvent and the wineserver's own wakes wake all too.

- A wake without ULF_WAKE_ALL goes to a thread still queued on the address; one that timed out or
  was interrupted by a signal (suspend, system APC) is already off the queue, so the wake goes to the
  next sleeper. A woken wait-any thread takes the object at once or finds it taken.
- Wait-all threads wait for each object in turn without taking it, so one could take the only wake
  and go to sleep on the next object, leaving the object free and the other waiters asleep. They now
  hand such a wake on (wake one more if the object is still free) before sleeping again, timing out
  or leaving for an APC. squark's tools/msync-test wakepass.exe: without the hand-on about half of the
  releases in its "absorb" part are lost.
- What a wait-all puts back after failing to take everything (tooslow) woke nobody, even with
  ULF_WAKE_ALL: a waiter that looked meanwhile slept with the object free until the next release. It
  now wakes one, the wineserver's list of multi-object waiters included.
- Waiters with an alert object or on several objects wait on the wineserver's list, which is still
  woken completely.
- SQUARK_MSYNC_WAKE_ALL=1 wakes all single-object waiters again (the put-back wake stays).

